(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-sleep-apnea-ohs-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Obstructive Sleep Apnea and Obesity Hypoventilation Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 47-year-old man with a body mass index of 38 kg/m2 has obstructive sleep apnea. Which additional finding should prompt screening for obesity hypoventilation syndrome?","choices":["Awake room-air saturation of 93%","Overnight lowest saturation of 86%","A serum bicarbonate in the normal range","Loud snoring with witnessed apneas"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Obese individuals, with or without obstructive sleep apnea, are screened when they have an unexplained awake room-air oxygen saturation of 94% or less or an overnight lowest under 80%, unexplained exertional dyspnea, signs of pulmonary hypertension or right heart failure, facial plethora or a raised bicarbonate. 93% awake qualifies. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 31, 30, 32"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman feels tired and sleepy most days, and her husband has seen her stop breathing and gasp during sleep. She does not snore loudly and is not treated for high blood pressure. Her body mass index is 37 kg/m2 and her neck (shirt collar) measures 15 inches. Using the STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) questionnaire, which result fits her?","choices":["Score 2, low risk","Score 3, intermediate risk","Score 3, high risk","Score 4, intermediate risk"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Snoring 0 + tired 1 + observed apnea 1 + pressure 0 + body mass index 37 (more than 35) 1 + age 46 0 + neck 15 inches 0 + female 0 = 3. By count that is intermediate, but yes to 2 or more of the first 4 questions plus a body mass index above 35 is a high-risk rule, so she is high risk. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 16, 17"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man with a body mass index of 39 kg/m2 has loud snoring and daytime sleepiness. His awake arterial carbon dioxide pressure is 43 mm Hg (normal 35-45), and no other cause of hypoventilation has been found. How do his results measure against the criteria for obesity hypoventilation syndrome?","choices":["Met: obesity with snoring is enough","Met: his body mass index exceeds 35","Not met: body mass index is too low","Not met: carbon dioxide not above 45"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis needs all three: obesity (body mass index above 30), awake alveolar hypoventilation with an arterial carbon dioxide pressure above 45 mm Hg, and exclusion of other causes. His body mass index of 39 qualifies, but 43 mm Hg is not above 45, so awake hypoventilation is not shown. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 32, 30, 17"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with a body mass index of 43 kg/m2, hypersomnolence and a raised serum bicarbonate is being evaluated for obesity hypoventilation syndrome. Which test is the gold standard for her?","choices":["Sleep study with oxygen saturation alone","Home sleep apnea test","Daytime arterial blood gas alone","Sleep study with carbon dioxide monitoring"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Polysomnography with continuous nocturnal carbon dioxide monitoring is the gold standard for obesity hypoventilation syndrome. Following carbon dioxide through the night captures the hypoventilation of the syndrome alongside the obstructive events that coexist in about 90% of patients. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 32, 31, 30, 8"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man with moderate obstructive sleep apnea has no teeth and wears full dentures. He cannot tolerate his continuous positive airway pressure mask and asks about a mouthpiece instead. Which option should be avoided?","choices":["Oral appliance","Radiofrequency ablation","Nasal expiratory resistance device","Positional therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An oral appliance is not effective in patients who have no teeth (or in severe obstructive sleep apnea). He is edentulous, so the mouthpiece he asks about is the option to avoid, even though he cannot tolerate his mask. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 12, 13"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman's baseline sleep recording shows mild to moderate sleep-disordered breathing. Her clinician wants to know how severe it is in different stages of sleep and in different sleep positions. Which study fits?","choices":["Split-night polysomnogram","Home sleep apnea kit","Pressure titration study","All-night polysomnogram"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The all-night polysomnogram is performed when sleep-disordered breathing is mild to moderate during the baseline, and it helps determine severity in different stages of sleep and in different sleep positions, the exact question her clinician is asking. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman is diagnosed with obesity hypoventilation syndrome. She has coexisting obstructive sleep apnea, and her awake room-air oxygen saturation is 85% (normal 95-100%). Which feature of her presentation points to a poor prognosis?","choices":["Significant awake hypoxemia","Her female sex","Her age in the 60s","Coexisting obstructive apnea"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Prognosis is poor when there is significant awake hypoxemia at diagnosis and with positive airway pressure therapy. Her awake room-air saturation of 85% is well below normal, and the syndrome already carries high mortality and morbidity, with cardiovascular disease the leading cause of death. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 35, 29, 30"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with heart failure has central sleep apnea with Cheyne-Stokes breathing (hyperventilation alternating with apnea) on polysomnography. His leg edema is worsening, his heart failure medicines have not been adjusted in a year, and he takes no opioids. What is the first step?","choices":["Optimize his heart failure therapy","Start adaptive servo-ventilation","Reduce opioid medications","Repeat with a home sleep test"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Central sleep apnea is treated by addressing the underlying cause first, and in heart failure the step is to optimize heart failure therapy. His worsening edema shows his heart failure is not controlled; continuous positive airway pressure is considered afterward, then adaptive servo-ventilation if it fails. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 26, 24"},{"q":"A 51-year-old man is evaluated before elective surgery. He snores loudly and is treated for high blood pressure. He feels rested during the day, and his wife has not seen him stop breathing. His body mass index is 35 kg/m2 and his neck (shirt collar) measures 17 inches. What is his STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) score?","choices":["Score of 4","Score of 5","Score of 6","Score of 7"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Snoring 1 + tired 0 + observed 0 + treated pressure 1 + body mass index 35 (not more than 35) 0 + age 51 (older than 50) 1 + neck 17 inches 1 + male 1 = 5, the high-risk band (5 to 8). He would also be high risk through 2 yes answers to the first 4 questions plus male gender. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 16, 17"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man has loud, habitual snoring, unrefreshing sleep, morning headaches and nocturia. His wife has seen him stop breathing and wake gasping. His blood pressure is poorly controlled on two medicines. His neck circumference is 18 inches, and his posterior oropharynx is crowded. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Narcolepsy","Obstructive sleep apnea","Central sleep apnea","Obesity hypoventilation syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Loud habitual snoring, witnessed apneas, awakening with gasping, unrefreshing sleep, morning headaches, nocturia and poorly controlled hypertension are its history findings, and a large neck and a crowded posterior oropharynx are its examination findings. Narrowing of the upper airway by soft tissue or structure causes it. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 5, 7, 10, 24, 25, 32"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man is having a split-night polysomnogram. The first 2 hours of sleep show significant obstructive sleep apnea. What happens during the rest of the night?","choices":["Adaptive servo-ventilation titration","Untreated recording until morning","Continuous positive airway pressure titration","Bilevel positive airway pressure titration"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In a split-night polysomnogram the first 2 hours of sleep are recorded to determine severity, and if obstructive sleep apnea is significant, the rest of the night is a continuous positive airway pressure titration that finds the exact pressure needed to keep the airway open. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 26, 33"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with heart failure reports insomnia, frequent awakenings and nocturnal dyspnea. His wife says he rarely snores, and his breathing at night shows Cheyne-Stokes breathing (hyperventilation alternating with apnea). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Obesity hypoventilation syndrome","Panic attacks","Central sleep apnea","Obstructive sleep apnea"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Heart failure is a risk factor for central sleep apnea, especially through Cheyne-Stokes breathing, hyperventilation alternating with apnea from increased sensitivity to carbon dioxide and a prolonged circulation delay to the carotid chemoreceptors. Insomnia, awakenings and nocturnal dyspnea fit, and snoring is less common than in obstructive apnea. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 24, 25, 10, 32"},{"q":"A 51-year-old woman with untreated obstructive sleep apnea has a fasting glucose of 118 mg/dL (normal 70-99) and triglycerides of 210 mg/dL (normal below 150). Which consequence of her sleep disorder do these results reflect?","choices":["Hypothyroidism caused by the apnea","Cortisol deficiency from poor sleep","Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease","Insulin resistance and type 2 diabetes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Patients with obstructive sleep apnea have increased prevalence of insulin resistance, type 2 diabetes and diabetes complications, and the disease is independently associated with increased glucose and triglyceride levels. It may also worsen cardiovascular risk in metabolic syndrome. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 20, 21"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman snores loudly, feels tired during the day and is treated for high blood pressure. No one has seen her stop breathing. Her body mass index is 34 kg/m2 and her neck (shirt collar) measures 15.5 inches. Using the STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) questionnaire, which result fits her?","choices":["Score 3, intermediate risk","Score 4, intermediate risk","Score 4, high risk","Score 5, high risk"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Snoring 1 + tired 1 + observed 0 + treated pressure 1 + body mass index 34 (not above 35) 0 + age 63 (older than 50) 1 + neck 15.5 inches 0 + female 0 = 4, the intermediate band (3 to 4). She has 3 yes answers to the first 4 questions, but none of the add-ons (male, body mass index above 35, neck 16 inches) applies. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 16, 17"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman has a body mass index of 29 kg/m2, daytime sleepiness and an awake arterial carbon dioxide pressure of 50 mm Hg (normal 35-45). How do her results measure against the criteria for obesity hypoventilation syndrome?","choices":["Met: hypercapnia alone defines it","Not met: carbon dioxide is too low","Not met: body mass index not above 30","Met: carbon dioxide is above 45"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Obesity hypoventilation syndrome requires obesity (body mass index above 30), awake hypoventilation with an arterial carbon dioxide pressure above 45 mm Hg, and exclusion of alternative causes. Her carbon dioxide of 50 qualifies, but a body mass index of 29 is not above 30, so the diagnosis cannot be made. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 32, 28"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with long-standing untreated obstructive sleep apnea has blood tests drawn. Which laboratory change is expected from his untreated disease?","choices":["Increased prolactin","Decreased cortisol","Increased C-reactive protein","Increased growth hormone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The endocrine effects of untreated obstructive sleep apnea include increased C-reactive protein, cortisol and amyloid, with decreased growth hormone and prolactin. Proinflammatory cytokines also rise, and they improve with continuous positive airway pressure. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 21, 22"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with loud snoring, witnessed apneas and daytime sleepiness is seen for evaluation before elective surgery. He has never been tested for sleep apnea. Which property makes the STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) questionnaire suited to this visit?","choices":["It grades his disease severity","Highest sensitivity of the questionnaires","Highest specificity of the questionnaires","It confirms the diagnosis before surgery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The questionnaire has been used as a preoperative evaluation tool for people without a known diagnosis of obstructive sleep apnea who may be at risk for perioperative complications, and it has the highest sensitivity among the questionnaires. A highly symptomatic patient in a high-risk setting is where it has value. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 16, 17, 8, 9"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man's polysomnogram shows repeated episodes, each 10 seconds or longer, of slowed, shallow, restricted breathing. Airflow is reduced but does not fall by 90%. How are these episodes classified?","choices":["Mixed apneas","Hypopneas","Obstructive apneas","Central apneas"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A hypopnea is slowed, shallow, restricted breathing that occurs repeatedly during sleep in episodes of 10 seconds or longer. An apnea needs a fall in airflow of 90% or more for at least 10 seconds, which his episodes do not reach, so these are hypopneas. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 38-year-old long-haul truck driver has untreated obstructive sleep apnea with marked daytime sleepiness and is reluctant to start treatment. What should he be told about his rate of driving accidents compared with people without the disorder?","choices":["2 to 3 times higher","2.5 times higher","3 times higher","3 to 6 times higher"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Untreated obstructive sleep apnea carries 2.5 times the rate of driving accidents, because daytime sleepiness increases motor vehicle crashes. For a man who drives for a living with marked sleepiness, that risk is a reason for counseling and treatment. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 18, 15, 20"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman with a neuromuscular disease causing generalized weakness has snoring and morning headaches. Her awake room-air oxygen saturation is 92% (normal 95-100%). Which study should evaluate her for sleep-disordered breathing?","choices":["Home sleep apnea kit","Repeat saturation in a month","Pressure titration study","Attended polysomnogram"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Neuromuscular weakness and awake hypoventilation or hypoxemia are both conditions in which a polysomnogram is used instead of an at-home kit. Her room-air saturation of 92% is below normal, and a polysomnogram can also find sleep disorders other than obstructive sleep apnea. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 31"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with obstructive sleep apnea uses continuous positive airway pressure nightly but still has daytime sleepiness. He also has coronary artery disease, cirrhosis and generalized anxiety disorder. Which treatment calls for caution because of his other conditions?","choices":["Positional therapy","Diet and exercise for weight loss","Modafinil","A nasal steroid spray"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Stimulants such as modafinil or armodafinil are used with caution in patients with cardiovascular, hepatic or psychiatric conditions, and he has all three: coronary artery disease, cirrhosis and an anxiety disorder. Nervousness and anxiety are also listed side effects. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 11, 12, 15"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with untreated obstructive sleep apnea has trouble falling asleep and asks for a sleeping pill. Which option should be avoided?","choices":["A sedative sleeping pill","Diet and exercise for weight loss","Referral to a sleep physician","Positional therapy with a sleep shirt"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Use of alcohol or sedatives is a risk factor for obstructive sleep apnea, and the risk modification is to avoid alcohol and other sedatives. A sedative sleeping pill would add a risk factor to his untreated apnea. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 15, 12"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man had a stroke last year. His wife reports loud snoring and pauses in his breathing during sleep, and a home sleep apnea kit has been ordered. What is the best next step?","choices":["Proceed with the home kit as ordered","Start positive airway pressure now","Prescribe modafinil for sleepiness","Change the order to a polysomnogram"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Stroke is one of the conditions in which a polysomnogram is used instead of an at-home kit. Stroke is also a risk factor for central sleep apnea, and at-home testing assesses obstructive sleep apnea and is not sufficient for central apnea, so the order should change. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 11, 12, 24, 26"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man who takes long-term opioid medications has central sleep apnea confirmed on polysomnography. He has no heart failure and lives at sea level. Which step treats the underlying cause?","choices":["Optimize heart failure therapy","Add a stimulant for sleepiness","Reduce the opioids","Start adaptive servo-ventilation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Central sleep apnea is treated first by addressing the underlying cause, and when the patient takes opioid medications, the step is to reduce the opioids. After that, continuous positive airway pressure is considered, and adaptive servo-ventilation if it fails. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 26, 24, 11"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man with obstructive sleep apnea has respiratory events on polysomnogram that occur when he sleeps on his back and not when he lies on his side. He wants a measure to add to his care. Which therapy targets this pattern?","choices":["Positional therapy","Nasal steroid spray","Palatal implants","Tracheostomy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Positional therapy fits when events occur in the supine position: a sleep belt or sleep shirt keeps him lying on his side. His events are confined to back-sleeping, the exact pattern it addresses. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 12, 13"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man started continuous positive airway pressure for obstructive sleep apnea 2 months ago. A repeat polysomnogram on the device shows repetitive apneas in the absence of thoracic-abdominal excursion. Which complication of his therapy has developed?","choices":["Central sleep apnea","Obstructive apnea despite therapy","Obesity hypoventilation syndrome","Sleep-related hypoventilation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients using continuous positive airway pressure are listed among those at risk for central sleep apnea. Repetitive apneas in the absence of thoracic-abdominal excursion mean airflow stops because effort stops, the polysomnogram finding of central apnea. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 24, 26, 3, 30"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man has a polysomnogram as part of a research study. He has no sleepiness, fatigue or insomnia, and no one has noted snoring or pauses in his breathing. The study shows 7 predominantly obstructive events per hour. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["Criteria for diagnosis are not met","Diagnostic of obstructive sleep apnea","Diagnostic of central sleep apnea","Needs positive airway pressure therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Without symptoms, the diagnosis needs 15 or more predominantly obstructive events per hour; 5 or more counts only with a symptom such as sleepiness, fatigue, insomnia, choking or snoring noted by an observer. He has 7 events and no symptoms, so neither criterion is met. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 9, 12, 26"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with obesity hypoventilation syndrome stopped her positive airway pressure therapy a year ago. She now has exertional dyspnea, an elevated jugular venous pressure, hepatomegaly and pedal edema. Which complication has developed?","choices":["Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease","Central sleep apnea","Insulin resistance","Right-sided heart failure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Obesity hypoventilation syndrome tends to progress if left untreated, and many patients develop cardiovascular complications, pulmonary hypertension and right-sided heart failure. Elevated jugular venous pressure, hepatomegaly and pedal edema are the listed signs of right-sided heart failure. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 35, 34, 31"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man on a mountain trek at high altitude wakes frequently at night. His tent partner notices that his breathing speeds up and then stops, over and over, without snoring. He has no heart failure and takes no medications. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Nocturnal asthma","Central sleep apnea","Obstructive sleep apnea","Panic attacks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. High altitude is a risk factor for central sleep apnea, in which increased sensitivity to carbon dioxide produces an unstable pattern of hyperventilation alternating with apnea. Frequent awakenings without snoring fit, since snoring is less common than in obstructive sleep apnea. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 24, 25, 10"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with a body mass index of 40 kg/m2 has obesity hypoventilation syndrome confirmed by exclusion. He has loud snoring, choking during sleep and resuscitative snorting, and polysomnography shows obstructive sleep apnea without sleep-related hypoventilation. Which first-line treatment fits him?","choices":["Tracheostomy with nocturnal ventilation","Weight-loss medication first","Weight loss plus continuous positive airway pressure","Weight loss plus bilevel positive airway pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. First-line therapy for obesity hypoventilation syndrome is a weight loss program plus positive airway pressure, and when the syndrome coexists with obstructive sleep apnea, the device is continuous positive airway pressure. His snoring, choking and resuscitative snorting are the obstructive symptoms seen in about 90% of patients. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 33, 30"}]};
